Clam is high-value seafood and is often cultured. It is popular in Aisa, the USA, and Caribbean.
Clam, Ruditapes philippinarum, is a marine bivalve mollusk indigenous to subtropical and temperate coastal seas of the western Pacific from the South China Sea north to the Sea of Okhotsk, as well as parts of the Indian Ocean. It is high-value seafood and is often cultured.
Clam occurs in coastal sediments from the inter-tidal zone to the shallow sub-littoral. The clam is reported as being typically found from the lower shore down but can occur on the upper shore in certain tidal regimes with prolonged high water stands.
Clam is an important shellfish both in China and in the rest of the world that is rich in nutrients, including proteins, vitamins, and essential elements and has become common in the human diet. It is famous for its thin shell, delicate meat, and delicious taste.
